WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.320
Hi I'm Jake Kitchener, I'm a Senior Technical&nbsp;
Staff Member here at IBM Cloud and the Lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.800
Architect for IBM Cloud Satellite, an exciting&nbsp;
product that we have available for you now in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.640
IBM Cloud. What is IBM Cloud Satellite? IBM Cloud&nbsp;
Satellite is a platform service that allows us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:21.040
expand the reaches of IBM Cloud to infrastructure&nbsp;
outside of IBM Cloud data centers. A powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.400
technology if you want to be able to consume&nbsp;
cloud services, but you need to be able to use it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.160
closer to your data, whether that may be in your&nbsp;
on-premises data center, on another cloud provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.800
where you may have a data lake, or potentially&nbsp;
it's sitting on your desk like I have right here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.280
Today I've got three Intel NUCs that I'm going&nbsp;
to be using to set up a Satellite location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.200
which I think is pretty cool. It's kind of like&nbsp;
any sort of store kiosk that you might need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:52.240
set up and it's a great example of the power of&nbsp;
using distributed cloud with IBM Cloud Satellite.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.520
So to get started, the first thing I need to do is&nbsp;
log into the IBM Cloud console and navigate over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.360
to IBM Cloud Satellite. Once I'm there, I'm going&nbsp;
to go ahead and create a new location to represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.200
my desk. I'm going to go ahead and click the&nbsp;
create location button, give this location a name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.760
and click create. Pretty straightforward. Now,&nbsp;
once that location is up and running what I need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:22.000
to do is bring some of my infrastructure on board.&nbsp;
To do that I'm going to navigate over to the Hosts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.200
section which is the concept that we use in IBM&nbsp;
Cloud Satellite to represent the idea of systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.120
that are outside of IBM Cloud as the host. The&nbsp;
first thing I need to do is click on Attach host.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.600
I'm going to go ahead and download this post&nbsp;
provisioning script and then once I've got that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.840
in my hand I can go apply that post provisioning&nbsp;
script to any system on the planet. That could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.480
in another cloud provider, it could be in your&nbsp;
on-premises data center, or in this case I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.680
to get it bootstrapped onto these Intel NUCs that&nbsp;
are sitting on my desk. Now to do that i've built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.560
a custom image that will automatically carve up&nbsp;
these systems into virtual machines and bootstrap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.320
them with my post provisioning script. So all&nbsp;
I need to do is get this onto that USB key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.880
insert it into my Intel NUCs, and go ahead&nbsp;
and power them on. Now I'm going to go ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.400
and go through that process for all three&nbsp;
of these because I want a highly available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.960
setup. So I can do maintenance by removing&nbsp;
these later on, but that's pretty much it.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.520
Now that those systems are online. We can see&nbsp;
they're all up here and ready for me to use in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.080
my Satellite location and I'm going to go ahead&nbsp;
and get one from each of those systems assigned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.960
to be my Satellite Control Plane. This is really&nbsp;
the heart of IBM Cloud Satellite that allows us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.280
to build a secure way for us to go manage cloud&nbsp;
services into your IBM Cloud Satellite location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.720
Now I've got these hosts assigned,&nbsp;
my location is in a normal state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.560
and I'm ready to start using IBM Cloud Services.&nbsp;
Let's go ahead and take a look at what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.080
experience is like. I'm going to navigate over to&nbsp;
the IBM Cloud Catalog and I could choose any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.640
the IBM Cloud Satellite enabled services that we&nbsp;
have available. Today we're going to demonstrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.440
Red Hat OpenShift on IBM Cloud, but it could be&nbsp;
other services as well like our Postgres database&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.720
or potentially maybe some of our Cloud Pak for&nbsp;
Data as a Service options as well. So let's go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.520
ahead into the catalog to Red Hat OpenShift&nbsp;
on IBM Cloud and we can see I still have my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.840
traditional cloud service options where I can&nbsp;
provision into IBM Cloud data centers like a VCP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.880
but I've also got the Satellite option available&nbsp;
to me as well. So I'm going to go ahead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.160
choose Satellite. Choose my new location I&nbsp;
created and go ahead and give that a name,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.200
and then create that OpenShift cluster.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.160
Now, I can navigate over to my OpenShift clusters&nbsp;
in IBM Cloud and we can see I've got a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.040
clusters in here, some of them are located in IBM&nbsp;
Cloud, some of them are in Satellite locations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.720
but this one is on my desk. Let's go ahead and&nbsp;
see what that looks like. I’m going to click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.840
over to my cluster here and I can see I’ve got all&nbsp;
the normal service options that I would have for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.320
Red Hat OpenShift and IBM Cloud, I can update the&nbsp;
version of this cluster to the latest version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.960
Red Hat OpenShift, or I can go in and perform&nbsp;
maintenance on my worker nodes as well like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.520
providing updates to them. I’m going to click&nbsp;
over to my cluster here and I can see I’ve got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.680
all the normal service options that I would have&nbsp;
for Red Hat OpenShift and IBM Cloud, I can update&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.158
the version of this cluster to the latest version&nbsp;
of Red Hat OpenShift, or I can go in and perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.680
maintenance on my worker nodes as well like&nbsp;
providing updates to them. I get the standard user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.600
experience with single sign-on from IBM Cloud into&nbsp;
Red Hat OpenShift and all the usual tools that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.560
would find in in Red Hat OpenShift are available -&nbsp;
I’ve got all my logging tools my monitoring tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.520
the registry, all of the development tools that&nbsp;
are available to users of Red Hat OpenShift.&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.800
So, very powerful tool that we've got available to&nbsp;
us here that allows us to really expand the reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.000
of IBM Cloud and the powerful Cloud services&nbsp;
that we provide - in this case on my desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.394
but remember, it could be on any infrastructure&nbsp;
in the world running Red Hat Enterprise Linux.

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:58.298
Thank you for watching. If you have questions, please drop us a line below.

